Fair question — let me draw the actual distinction, because "agent" gets thrown around loosely.

What Claude.ai / Claude Code already are

Claude.ai is a chat interface — you talk, it responds, in-conversation. Claude Code is already an agent in the technical sense: it can read files, run bash commands, edit code, loop on its own until a task is done, without you approving every micro-step. So Claude Code isn't "not an agent" — it's Anthropic's pre-built agent, scoped mostly around coding/dev workflows on your machine.

What "build my own agent" actually means

It's not really about intelligence — it's about control over the loop and the environment. When you build your own agent (with LangGraph, CrewAI, Claude Agent SDK, etc.), you're deciding:

What tools it has access to — not just files/bash like Claude Code, but anything: your Gmail, a specific API, your job scraper's SQLite DB, a scheduling system, a Slack channel. Claude Code can't check LinkedIn for you or auto-send outreach emails on a cron job — a custom agent can, because you wire that access in yourself.
What triggers it — Claude.ai/Code run only when you're actively chatting. Your own agent can run unattended: on a schedule (check for new job postings every hour), on an event (new email arrives), or as a background daemon.
What "done" means — you define the loop's exit condition, how many steps it can take, what it stores in memory between runs, and what happens on failure. In Claude.ai, you're the loop — you decide when to send the next message.
Where it lives — Claude Code runs in your terminal/desktop, tied to your session. A custom agent can run on a server, in Docker, continuously, independent of you having the app open.

Concretely, for something like your job scraper:
Right now (as I understand it) your scraper runs, finds jobs, and you review/act on them manually with Claude's help drafting outreach. A custom agent version could: scrape → filter by relevance itself → draft the outreach message → maybe even queue it for your approval or auto-send it → log what it did → repeat on a schedule, with no chat session open at all.

So the real question is: do you want something that acts while you're not there, or reaches into more systems than a chat/coding assistant naturally does? That's the signal for "build your own agent" vs. just using Claude Code more.

