is the MIT thing free?

Yes, completely free — MIT is just a license text, not a paid service or registration of any kind.

What it actually is

A short, standard block of legal text (a few sentences) that you paste into a LICENSE file in your repo. It says, roughly: anyone can use, copy, modify, and even sell software built with this code, as long as they keep the original copyright notice — and the original author isn't liable if something breaks. That's the whole thing.

No cost, ever, because:

No registration, no application, no governing body to pay
Not tied to GitHub specifically — it's a generic open-source license text that predates GitHub by decades
GitHub just offers it as a one-click template when you create a repo, purely for convenience — you're not "buying" anything, just selecting which pre-written legal text to drop in

Why it's the common default for projects like yours

It's the most permissive, simplest, and most widely recognized open-source license — recruiters/engineers skimming your GitHub will recognize it instantly and know exactly what they can do with your code, no reading required. That's exactly the frictionless "here, use this" signal you want for a portfolio piece meant to be seen and understood quickly.

So: pick it in the GitHub UI dropdown when creating the repo, it auto-generates the LICENSE file with your name and the year filled in, done — zero cost, zero further action needed.
